  "worked_solution": "To find out how many pages she can read in $10$ minutes, we need to multiply $\\frac{3}{4}$ by $10.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n10\\times \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{10\\times 3}{4}=\\frac{30}{4}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{4}=\\frac{30÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{15}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{15}{2}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15÷2=7\\,\\text{R}\\,1=7\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in $10$ minutes, Sandra can read a total of $7\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ pages of the book, which lies between $7$ and $8$ pages.",
